<body>
<main>
<section class="hero"><span class="badge">AI Product Management Certification &middot; Final Project</span><h1>Juno PM</h1><p class="pitch">Juno PM is a AI tool for Senior PMs to use to help identify what problems to solve next for RocketShip</p><p class="who">Adam Maslac · Class 6 · Oct 29, 2026</p><a class="cta" href="https://github.com/Maz-87/juno-pm/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View the repo &rarr;</a></section>
<section><div class="eyebrow">What I built</div><h2>Five modules, one AI Associate PM</h2><p class="lede">Each module added one piece. Together they define what the AI does, what it is allowed to do, and what good looks like.</p><div class="grid"><div class="m-card" style="--col:#F59A3F"><div class="m-head"><div class="m-num">Module 1</div><div class="m-name">Skill file</div></div><div class="m-body"><div class="m-row"><div class="m-label">Role</div><p>You are Juno PM, an AI Associate PM embedded in RocketShip's Slack, Notion, and Jira. Your job is to assist the Senior PM by highlighting risks, using data to identify opportunities, and assisting in spec writing once a feature has been accepted by the senior PM. You do not execute tasks autonomously.</p></div><div class="m-row"><div class="m-label">Task</div><p>- Synthesize signals into key themes.<br>- Each insight should have a count of the number of requests which support it and external links to the ticket reference.<br>- Insights should be priortised based on alignment with the most recent version of the company roadmap.</p></div></div></div><div class="m-card" style="--col:#DE8B80"><div class="m-head"><div class="m-num">Module 2</div><div class="m-name">Prototype</div></div><div class="m-body"><div class="m-row"><div class="m-label">Prototype</div><p><a href="https://id-preview--ef8e7f4a-267a-4f02-8a8f-074498c81bc2.lovable.app/table?__lovable_receipt=mat_2jket9b4829e2a4avnj8ny3yfn" target="_blank" rel="noopener">https://id-preview--ef8e7f4a-267a-4f02-8a8f-074498c81bc2.lovable.app/table?__lovable_receipt=mat_2jket9b4829e2a4avnj8ny3yfn</a></p></div><div class="m-row"><div class="m-label">What it demonstrates</div><p>This demonstrates an example of an 'Insight' tool that can be used by the PM Lead when preparing what to design/build/solve for in the upcoming sprint. It aims to remove the manual work required to sythesise feature requests manually by problem statements (rather than solution focused).</p></div><div class="m-row"><div class="m-label">Debrief</div><p>What worked:<br>- AI gave a great output with very little prompting on expected format<br>- Part of my prompt was for AI to ask any clarifying questions before proceeding<br>- AI successfully interated on the design and transposed it into a Notion style table (which was requested since this is one of the tools used by Juno)<br><br>What broke / felt like a toy:<br>- No links (since it is using simulated data)<br>- No ability to filter the table (eg. by source, customer etc) since the prototype isn't actually using Notion's native functionality<br>- Also unsure if hese accordion style grouping is possible within Notion (?)<br><br>What I'd change next pass:<br>- I would simplify the design to make it more digestable for the Lead PM. This could be done by being clearer on how the PM will use this tool.</p></div></div></div><div class="m-card" style="--col:#C77BC0"><div class="m-head"><div class="m-num">Module 3</div><div class="m-name">AI PRD</div></div><div class="m-body"><div class="m-row"><div class="m-label">Problem and user</div><p>RocketShip PMs need this handled: Juno maintains a view that synthesises and summarises all existing feature requests into common themes based on their problem statements.. Juno does this inside limits a human set on purpose, and defends every call it makes.</p></div><div class="m-row"><div class="m-label">Permissions</div><p>The permission tier<br><br>| Side-effect class | Tier |<br>| read | auto |<br>| draft | blocked |<br>| write | confirm |<br>| send | blocked |<br><br>Justification<br><br>It can read Slack and JIRA (but it cannot write).<br><br>It can read and write to the Notion dashboard/table</p></div></div></div><div class="m-card" style="--col:#A78BFA"><div class="m-head"><div class="m-num">Module 4</div><div class="m-name">Agent control panel</div></div><div class="m-body"><div class="m-row"><div class="m-label">What it is doing</div><p>Synthesize insights from feature requests into key themes/problem statements and output into a Notion table</p></div><div class="m-row"><div class="m-label">Rules of engagement</div><p>Agency Permission:<br><br>Juno can read JIRA (but it cannot write).<br>Juno can read Slack and only write to '#product-management' channel<br>Juno can read and write to the Notion dashboard/table<br><br>Access Control:<br><br>JIRA, Slack, Notion<br><br>Fallback Protocols:<br><br>Checkpoints:</p></div></div></div><div class="m-card" style="--col:#A9BCFF"><div class="m-head"><div class="m-num">Module 5</div><div class="m-name">Human eval rubric</div></div><div class="m-body"><div class="m-row"><div class="m-label">What I score</div><p>Juno's feature request summary. synthesise and summarise all existing feature requests into common themes based on their problem statements saving PMs time and effort.</p></div><div class="m-row"><div class="m-label">Pass bar</div><p>An average of 3 on the accuracy scale and a 'yes' to all other rubric questions<br><br>Fails outright, whatever the average:<br><br>- Hallucinations: any &quot;no&quot;<br>- Private Data: any &quot;no&quot;</p></div></div></div></div></section>
<section><div class="eyebrow">Module 6</div><h2>How I&rsquo;ll know it&rsquo;s working</h2><p class="lede">The goal the AI is meant to move, where it stands today, and the result that would make me stop.</p><div class="goal"><div class="mt-label">The goal</div><p>The time spent by PMs reviewing feature requests and synthesising commonalities into problem statements should be greatly reduced. This should allow PMs to spent more time solving the defined problems and delivering value for customers and RocketShip.</p></div><div class="mt-grid"><div class="mt-card" style="--col:#F59A3F"><div class="mt-label">Baseline</div><p>The goal metric should be to reduce how many hours per week PMs spend collectively reviewing and synthesising feature requests to deliver the same output as Juno. Currently this is approx 20 hours combined for 2 PMs, which equates to $2000 USD (20 hours x PM rate of $100 per hour)</p></div><div class="mt-card" style="--col:#C77BC0"><div class="mt-label">Target and review date</div><p>The goal metric should be to reduce the time spent by 90%. This figure of 90% was selected (rather than 100%) as 10% of time will still be required to review low context feature requests and evaluate Juno's output. This goal metric will be reviewed 60 days from implementing Juno.</p></div><div class="mt-card" style="--col:#A78BFA"><div class="mt-label">Usage metric</div><p>PMs are actively engaging with the Notion dashboard and spend less time analysing the source feature requests. The synthesis output quality should also be comparable to the manual work previously conducted by PMs.</p></div><div class="mt-card" style="--col:#A9BCFF"><div class="mt-label">Pattern I&rsquo;ll watch for first</div><p>PMs are spending significant time validating the insights/problem statements geneated by Juno PM</p></div></div><div class="stop"><div class="mt-label">Stop line</div><p>We will cease using AI for this task if after the defined 60 day period the cost of using AI is greater than or equal to $1,800 USD per week and the quality of the output is not comparable to the manual synthesis previously conducted by PMs</p></div></section>
<section><div class="eyebrow">Reflection</div><h2>What I learned</h2><p class="lede">Where it was hardest, what I&rsquo;ll keep doing, and the moment my thinking changed.</p><div class="in-grid"><div class="in-card" style="--col:#F59A3F"><div class="in-label">Friction</div><p>Understanding jargons around new AI terminology. The glossary of terms created by the instructor towards the end of the course was helpful, but this would have been useful earlier as a reference.</p></div><div class="in-card" style="--col:#C77BC0"><div class="in-label">Key learnings</div><p>Using evals and rubrics</p></div><div class="in-card" style="--col:#A9BCFF"><div class="in-label">Aha moment</div><p>The valuable skillset of a PM is no longer in the details of synthesising data, creating prototypes, or writing specifications. Instead it is higher level startegy functions such as stakeholder management, choosing the right things to build etc</p></div></div></section>
<footer>Adam Maslac · Class 6 · Oct 29, 2026 &middot; AI Product Management Certification &middot; Final Project</footer>
